afety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":27,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The supervisor, notes and predictions are in `work/`. I couldn't run the simulator, so its effect on TTS is a reasoned estimate. On the recorded episodes it behaves almost exactly like the naive shield.\n\n**What it does:** `supervisor.py` uses only the logits and the rule state. When only one phase is legal, it returns that phase. Otherwise it takes the legal phase the network scores highest. When the network's own choice is forbidden, that replaces the shield's \"most halting vehicles\" pick.\n\n**When it overrides:** only when the network's top two legal options are within 1.0 logit of each other:\n- it serves a phase now if that phase would otherwise become overdue and be forced in at the next switch;\n- it prefers a left phase to a through phase, because a through green is now held to 17 s. In all 645 recorded through holds, that phase had no halting vehicles left by 12 s.\n\nIt differs from the naive shield at 48 of 4,437 recorded decisions (1.1%). It differs from the network's raw choice 66% of the time, almost all of it holds the rules force.\n\n**Checks:** on the recorded states, every output is legal, and all 48 overrides were inspected by hand. Different input types, missing state and edge cases all pass. Most of the 2.5 veh-h loss comes from that forced hold, which no supervisor can remove.\n\n**Predictions:** low 36.0, nominal 46.9, high 57.8 veh-h. That is about 0.1–0.2 below naive. It could plausibly come in up to 0.2 worse than naive.\n\nFiles are in `work/`:\n- supervisor.py\n- NOTES.md\n- predictions.json","ttft_ms":1339,"type":"result","duration_ms":651631,"uuid":"0b670162-9a0b-463a-bc28-176e07afc701","ttft_stream_ms":1190,"time_to_request_ms":71,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":3,"system_prompt":7,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":1,"input_attachments":8,"input_hooks":1,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":5,"tool_pool_refresh":2,"